{"id": "fs_155", "fact": "Learned B+-Tree indexing (SIGMOD 2026) replaces binary search inside B-tree nodes with tiny neural networks that fit entirely in L1 cache, skipping dozens of branch mispredictions per lookup. A principled algorithmic advance — not AI hype — that could land in production databases within a year. Insight: traditional index structures' 'data-distribution-independent' property is itself a performance ceiling when data has learnable patterns.", "source": "Hacker News SIGMOD 2026 paper discussion (2026-05-19)", "date": "2026-05-19", "tags": "persistent,databases,algorithms,AI,indexing,performance", "confidence": 0.88}

